==============================================================================
WHERE THE SHELL SITS
==============================================================================
  Delta = 2 Lambda_shell exp(-1/lambda),  1/lambda = 33.474, Delta = 3152.0 GeV
  -> Lambda_shell = 5.434e+17 GeV
  electroweak scale v = 246.0 GeV
  the shell sits 15.3 orders above it -- deep in the UNBROKEN phase

==============================================================================
THE ROSTER, WITH BOTH CHARGE ASSIGNMENTS
==============================================================================
  field                n       Y     n*Y^2   sum Q^2
  --------------------------------------------------
  Q_L  (3,2,1/6)       6  0.1667    0.1667    1.6667
  u_R  (3,1,2/3)       3  0.6667    1.3333    1.3333
  d_R  (3,1,-1/3)      3 -0.3333    0.3333    0.3333
  L    (1,2,-1/2)      2 -0.5000    0.5000    1.0000
  e_R  (1,1,-1)        1 -1.0000    1.0000    1.0000
  nu_R (1,1,0)         1  0.0000    0.0000    0.0000
  --------------------------------------------------
  per generation      16            3.3333    5.3333
  x 3 generations     48           10.0000   16.0000

  Cross-check on the hypercharge sum, which is what the unbroken phase screens with:
    b_Y = (2/3) sum_Weyl Y^2 + (1/3) sum_scalar Y^2 = 6.83333   = 41/6 = 6.83333
    the Standard Model's own hypercharge beta coefficient, so sum Y^2 = 10 is right.
  And sum Q^2 = 16, the value the finiteness table records.

==============================================================================
IS ANY SPECIES VECTOR-LIKE? (does a Dirac cone exist for mu_5 to sit on?)
==============================================================================
  left-handed  (SU(2) doublets): Q_L  (3,2,1/6), L    (1,2,-1/2)
  right-handed (SU(2) singlets): u_R  (3,1,2/3), d_R  (3,1,-1/3), e_R  (1,1,-1), nu_R (1,1,0)

  pairs sharing (Y, SU(2) rep, colour): 0
  Every left-handed field is a doublet and every right-handed one a singlet, so the SU(2)
  representation alone forbids a vector-like pair -- the hypercharges never even get a
  chance to match. That is what 'the Standard Model is a chiral gauge theory' means, and
  it is exactly the obstruction: there is no Dirac cone in the unbroken phase.

==============================================================================
WHAT THIS DOES TO THE SELECTION ARGUMENT
==============================================================================
  The charge-weighted selection -- one doped cone worth 2 N_c q^2, hence a charged lepton
  -- is a BROKEN-phase computation. It uses electric charge, and it uses the electron's
  two chiralities as one cone. Both become available only below the electroweak scale,
  15 orders below where section 6c puts the shell. Held at the shell it does not apply;
  held in the broken phase it applies but the shell is in the wrong place.

  So the selection result stands as a CONDITIONAL: if the medium's screening happens in
  the broken phase, the doped pair must be a charged lepton. Whether it does is section
  6f's open fork, and this is a third horn of it -- sharper than the coupling-running
  horn, because it is not about what value alpha takes but about whether the objects the
  kernel refers to exist at that scale at all.

  The unbroken-phase reading of the same requirement is worth stating: the screened
  charge is hypercharge, the whole roster doped is sum Y^2 = 10 rather than 16, and
  N_screen = 2 N_0 would have to be met by hypercharge multiplets rather than by a cone.
